# Configuration for the unit this package installs (esidx.service).
#
# systemd reads this as an environment file, not as shell: one KEY=value per line, no
# command substitution, no quoting unless a value needs it. A change here needs
# `systemctl restart esidx` to take effect.
#
# One process reads all of it. That is the whole shape now: the server opens the fanotify
# group itself, gives the privilege back before it opens the listener, and answers ETP from
# the same process -- so "the privileged half and the unprivileged half were configured into
# looking at different trees" stopped being a failure this file can cause. The two-process
# form still exists (run sfa-server separately and pass --watch=SOCK), and then ESIDX_ROOT has
# to be named in both places; see README, "Deployment".

# The locations to index are NOT here. They are in /etc/esidx/roots, one absolute path per
# line, and that is where you add or remove one -- see the comments in that file for why a
# list cannot be a KEY=value. This file needs a restart; the roots file does not: the unit
# passes --reload-config=/etc/esidx/roots, so an edit there is picked up live (a stat every
# 5 s, then a rebuild in a child process and a swap without a restart). The list itself
# still reaches the server through the snapshot -- `--watch-embed` takes it from there, so
# there is no second place for it to be wrong. The two-process form does need it twice (the
# proxy's --prefix, and what esidx is told to accept), which is the one place this deployment
# still has to keep two things in step; see README, "Deployment".

# The snapshot. Nothing builds this for you at install time -- the first one is a full scan,
# which does not belong in a maintainer script -- and nothing has to: the server starts
# without it and builds it itself, as ESIDX_USER, the first time it finds that /etc/esidx/
# roots and the snapshot disagree (design D11), which on a fresh install they do. It is
# therefore usually absent for the first minute of the service's life and present after it.
#
# The snapshot must stay readable by ESIDX_USER, and writable by it if
# --save is on. That is arranged with the group rather than with ownership: the directory is
# owned by the dedicated user and shared with the dedicated group (2775, postinst), and
# ESIDX_USER is put in that group. Three identities write here -- the unit's process before it
# drops, the user it becomes, and the admin who builds snapshots -- and a directory can belong
# to only one of them.
#
# Keep this path off /tmp. The unit sets PrivateTmp=yes, so its /tmp is a private mount and
# the host's /tmp is not visible inside it -- put the snapshot there and the unit dies at
# startup with status=226/NAMESPACE, which is a namespace error and not a hint about /tmp.
# (That is not hypothetical: it is what happened when the unit was first tested.)
ESIDX_DB=/var/lib/esidx/root.idx

# ETP over TCP. 0.0.0.0 listens on every interface, which is the default because a service whose
# whole job is to answer searches from other machines cannot only answer on its own; what keeps
# that safe is IPAddressAllow= in esidx.service (loopback + the private ranges, enforced by the
# kernel per unit). Set 127.0.0.1 to take it off the network entirely instead.
#
# Read that line before widening the allow list: ETP clients here authenticate anonymously, so
# the address list is the only access control, and anything it admits can enumerate every
# filename under ESIDX_ROOT.
ESIDX_PORT=2121
ESIDX_BIND=0.0.0.0

# The user the server becomes once the fanotify group is open. It starts as root because
# CAP_SYS_ADMIN is needed to open the group, and it keeps exactly one capability afterwards
# (CAP_DAC_READ_SEARCH, for turning an event into a path); everything else is dropped, and
# test_watch.sh asserts both halves of that from outside the process.
#
# The unit states this explicitly, but the binary would derive the same answer with
# `--drop-to` omitted: the default is the snapshot's owner, which is who `esidx build` ran as
# (postinst below). Point it at another user only if that user can read ESIDX_ROOT and
# ESIDX_DB -- and note that root is refused either way, so a snapshot built by root fails
# loudly instead of producing a privileged service.
ESIDX_USER=esidx

# How often to walk the directory stamps and reconcile anything the events could not carry:
# a file edited in place moves nothing its parent's mtime can see, and an event the proxy
# could not resolve is announced rather than described. 3600 is an hour. 0 disables it.
ESIDX_SWEEP=3600
